    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm trying to sort out some notes for a client regarding their inheritance tax situation. they have a property in spain and some liquid assets held in a trust in jersey, plus most of their estate is UK-based. can you give me a quick rundown of how residency usually affects the tax liability on those cross-border assets? specifically looking for how the UK's domicile rules might interact with the foreign holdings.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Residency can significantly impact inheritance tax liabilities across borders. In your client's case, if they are considered a resident for tax purposes in the UK, their worldwide assets, including their Spanish property, would typically be subject to UK inheritance tax. The UK allows for certain exemptions or reliefs, perhaps depending on their residential status for a certain period. Dicey a UK domicile will also play a role in the tax treatment of the foreign estate. They must confirm whether any tax treaties exist between the UK and Spain or Jersey which could affect the tax liability, as these treaties can provide mutual tax benefits or exemptions.",
